#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079ed11e-518b-573c-aa01-ad6a596181cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561606} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb43d11-d981-5b1d-a441-dc7bb7487b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561658} {:answer/id #uuid "b64dbb57-aa2e-57ce-8ba1-baf93f0d0a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562036} {:answer/id #uuid "be43a691-2e56-56b2-9c4c-68d6f001fcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562068} {:answer/id #uuid "c0474143-18b2-5d56-a3ca-e652dce65abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0474143-18b2-5d56-a3ca-e652dce65abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562069} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b92d65-4457-5e1c-8c99-2be1b9018bda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b70ed03e-6463-5d38-ae5e-1f3dea3b3eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104053" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8131b2ca68e378ef96c1b57e655db0a89d6e5207f0ac842612c64b30a963912.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $1,$ $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(1)=-12.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the candidates are  $x=-2,-1,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-12 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2) \\\\  & =-12+2 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12-(-2+1) \\\\  & =-12+1 \\\\  & =-11 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12-5 \\\\  & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-12,-10,-11,-17\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is  ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68ad5e9-7fe7-596a-8c2b-19ee3b81de60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185785} {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} {:answer/id #uuid "23daea13-16e4-5115-a876-009e017926b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185787} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24e81a-7cd0-52ae-a4ec-7a2d4936cd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185788} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecf83c7-e917-54b8-9b79-79ccd5e0603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15178949-e15f-5295-b833-173787b1d3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185786} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb7bcb5-fc17-5e6e-81c2-7dacca31243a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c577835a-f79d-5fd0-ba9c-ce150ab9e53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144828" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function comprises $2$ triangles, $2$ rectangles, and $1$ trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/99/99f558b3c1d86c5638543361a63823f13a9e01f2cc5042d0f781080f9f33c8a2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+2+4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}\\}=\\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{19}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae9fb4d-e189-5423-8d13-3a85f24bfe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae9fb4d-e189-5423-8d13-3a85f24bfe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562089} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d9913c-f4ac-5073-baaf-6eb787330138" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69626226-2183-5bc4-b118-9ee330e48ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344169" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a971f2e8581ad9a623a8c8541ee1fcae5911fa0d3ae1b91923d19612d74ec9c.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-7,-1].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $14.$\n\nGiven that $s(-1)=3,$ what is the minimum value ${s}_{\\text{min}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[-7,-1]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $s(x)$ on $[-7,-1]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-7,-1),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-7,-1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(-1) & =3 \\\\ s(-7) & =s(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-7}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =s(-1)-{\\int }_{-7}^{-1}{s}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(-14) \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{17,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f13395-bb5b-5cb7-9ee3-5858a0482aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f13395-bb5b-5cb7-9ee3-5858a0482aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562031} :answer-field/id #uuid "64590d23-2a3d-57b0-ad84-82063765d606" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c6e54a6-aeaf-5247-9068-bf68741c13b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344177" :question/problem "![](images/75/7571f7cb43a21a8369fc9b8197c25788a4005242d373aba2ffce44e7f6221cd6.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $8$ and $3,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $g(2)=4,$ what is the minimum value ${g}_{\\text{min}}$ of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]?$\n\n${g}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $g(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**:  Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & =4 \\\\ g(-1) & =g(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-3) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(-6) & =g(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(8+(-3)) \\\\  & =4-5 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,7,-1\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{min}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e9434cb-bb9c-5324-9bf4-b108c900c3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e9434cb-bb9c-5324-9bf4-b108c900c3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561973} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a41a06a-2eac-576e-969d-bc422a8f4058" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfdcc57a-5fbc-57f0-b88e-30c636d05052" :question/math-academy-id "q-344468" :question/problem "![](images/36/36a84f32380e5735b130f6b8fce26655738a0c86b210a732893dc8500d9cbbf8.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,5]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/cf/cf8e3aa5a6000966c44b36071b2269540578cf123a19024a20a62fb61896dc51.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3-(-16) \\\\  & =13 \\\\ f(5) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-3,13,1\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=2.$"}]}
